1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,696
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,757 --> 00:00:10,087
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:10,087 --> 00:00:10,813
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,874 --> 00:00:14,418
Welcome to Unit 9: Pressure and Diffusion.

5
00:00:15,479 --> 00:00:21,234
Deep underground, heat builds up pressure that pushes hot water and steam towards the surface,

6
00:00:21,234 --> 00:00:27,534
where we can use its geothermal energy for electricity. In this unit, we'll explore pressure in solids,

7
00:00:27,534 --> 00:00:31,229
liquids and gases, and how particles spread out by diffusion.

8
00:00:32,289 --> 00:00:35,682
Pressure is the amount of force acting on a given area.

9
00:00:35,682 --> 00:00:41,679
When you pump up a bicycle tyre, the air inside presses on the inner surface, making the tyre firm.

10
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:48,797
We calculate pressure by dividing the force by the area. It's measured in newtons per square metre.

11
00:00:48,797 --> 00:00:52,795
The triangle helps us rearrange: force equals pressure times area.

12
00:00:53,856 --> 00:00:57,975
A rock weighing 5000 newtons rests on an area of 0.5 square metres.

13
00:00:57,975 --> 00:01:02,458
The pressure is 5000 divided by 0.5: 10 thousand newtons per square metre.

14
00:01:03,518 --> 00:01:09,273
Now the other way: a truck exerts 10 thousand newtons per square metre over 0.4 square metres.

15
00:01:09,273 --> 00:01:12,242
The force is 10 thousand times 0.4: 4000 newtons.

16
00:01:13,302 --> 00:01:17,906
The same force can give very different pressures. When you press a pushpin,

17
00:01:17,906 --> 00:01:23,842
the force from your thumb is spread over the broad head, but it's concentrated on the tiny point.

18
00:01:23,842 --> 00:01:28,325
The smaller the area, the greater the pressure, so the pin goes in easily.

19
00:01:29,386 --> 00:01:35,868
Knives and axes have a very thin cutting edge, a small area, so they exert a high pressure and cut easily.

20
00:01:35,868 --> 00:01:41,319
As they're used, the edge gets wider and blunt, so we sharpen them to keep the area small.

21
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:48,074
Skis, snowboards and surfboards do the opposite: their large area spreads the rider's weight,

22
00:01:48,074 --> 00:01:50,619
reducing the pressure so they don't sink.

23
00:01:50,619 --> 00:01:56,010
A 700 newton skier on 1.4 square metres of skis exerts just 500 newtons per square metre.

24
00:01:57,070 --> 00:01:59,675
Two identical bricks have the same weight.

25
00:01:59,675 --> 00:02:04,400
One lies flat on a block of soft modelling clay; the other stands on its end.

26
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:08,641
Predict: which brick will sink deeper into the clay? Pause and decide.

27
00:02:09,701 --> 00:02:13,457
Watch. Both bricks press down on the clay with the same force.

28
00:02:14,518 --> 00:02:20,030
The brick standing on its end makes a much deeper dent. The flat brick hardly sinks at all.

29
00:02:21,091 --> 00:02:27,209
Explain what you saw. The force is the same, but the brick on its end pushes on a much smaller area.

30
00:02:27,209 --> 00:02:32,176
Pressure equals force divided by area, so a smaller area gives a bigger pressure,

31
00:02:32,176 --> 00:02:36,659
and the clay is pushed in further. Pause and explain it in your own words.
